The Translation Ranking model optimizes the triplet margin loss (Schroff et al., 2015) in relation to the source and reference. Specifically, the loss is defined as:

L(χ) = L(s, h⁺, h⁻) + L(r, h⁺, h⁻)

where

L(s, h⁺, h⁻) = max{0, d(s, h⁺) − d(s, h⁻) + ϵ}

L(r, h⁺, h⁻) = max{0, d(r, h⁺) − d(r, h⁻) + ϵ}

Here, d(u, v) denotes the Euclidean distance between embeddings u and v, and ϵ is a margin. The model is trained so that the distance between the anchors (source s and reference r) and the "worse" hypothesis h⁻ is greater by at least ϵ than the distance to the "better" hypothesis h⁺ (Page 4).
